I focus on the node labeled "gene" (discrete, 5 possible states), which
should be dependent on the node labeled "cond" (discrete, 3 possible
states). This conditional relationship is also mentioned in the .net
file ("potential ( gene | cond )"). Yet the table does not look very
convincing, as neither rows (as i would expect) nor columns sum up to
1.0. By looking further into this in R, i found the following (nw.best
is the network i actually exported using savenet()):

> nw.best$nodes$gene$prob
                 c         t        c+t        c-t          0
Lin     0.4992636 0.4876923 0.61131611 0.07815631 0.07977528
Neutral 0.3843888 0.4123077 0.31365314 0.28657315 0.29887640
Stab    0.1163476 0.1000000 0.07503075 0.63527054 0.62134831

So there i have colsums 1.0. But to correctly reflect the relationship
(also shown as arrow from "cond" to "gene" in the plot of nw.best) there
should be rowsums, if i am not mistaken. Any ideas where i am wrong or
if this indeed is a wrong calculation of deal?
The exporting method obviously swaps the table around (columns become
rows, but there are only 3...). I already know, that the .net format
allows for continuous specification of such tables, but nevertheless, as
i understand bayesian networks, there should be 5 consecutive entries
summing up to 1.0, to reflect the actual dependency.

thanks

markus
